{
  "beruf": "einzelhandel",
  "title": "Shops and supermarkets",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Supermarkets and shops are one of the biggest employers in France, for the till, the shelves and the stockroom. This deck covers the shop and its parts, what you do during a shift, offers and refunds, safety and security, and customers and team. Customers are addressed with vous; colleagues often say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a shop or supermarket in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Hôte de caisse / hôtesse de caisse",
    "Employé de libre-service / employée de libre-service",
    "Vendeur / vendeuse",
    "Employé de rayon / employée de rayon",
    "Manager de rayon"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At the till you say vous to every customer: « Bonjour madame, vous avez la carte du magasin ? » Colleagues usually say tu, and many shops use first names.",
    "The questions you will repeat all day: « Vous voulez un sac ? », « Vous payez par carte ou en espèces ? », « Vous voulez le ticket ? »",
    "If a customer is unhappy and you are not sure what to do, do not argue: « Un instant, je vais chercher mon responsable. »",
    "Ask for your contract, your weekly schedule (planning) and your payslip in writing, and ask what safety training the shop gives. Rules on hours, breaks, Sunday work and pay differ by contract and collective agreement, so ask the employer, France Travail or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
